            "A cache trades a stated amount of staleness for a measured amount of speed: place it by who computes the value and how stale it may be, choose TTL versus explicit invalidation versus CDC-driven refresh deliberately, and design the three named failures — penetration (negative caching), breakdown (jitter, single-flight, stale-while-revalidate), and outage (degrade or fail fast, decided in advance). A queue trades immediacy for decoupling: assume at-least-once, build idempotent consumers whose side effect and dedup record commit in one transaction, bound the queue with an explicit backpressure policy, alert on consumer lag and DLQ depth, and wire dead-letters to an owner and a replay procedure. Both tools are trades with characteristic failures — name them, design the response, and drill it — because a cache or queue whose failure mode was never written down is an outage waiting on a clock.",
